    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 3,
      "name": "California",
      "item": "https://smallestateform.com/transfer-on-death-deed/california/"
    }
  ]
}`;
---

<StatePage
  title="California Transfer on Death Deed: Complete Guide (2026)"
  description="California Transfer on Death Deed avoids probate for real property under Civil Code §5600–5696. How to complete, record, and revoke a CA TOD Deed. Fees by county."
  canonicalUrl="https://smallestateform.com/transfer-on-death-deed/california/"
  jsonLd={[articleSchema, faqSchema, howToSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>

  <!-- Planning document callout -->
  <div class="bg-[#F0EDE8] border-l-4 border-[#B8860B] p-4 mb-4 max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8">
    <strong>Important:</strong> This is a PLANNING document — set it up now, takes effect at your death. It is NOT an after-death affidavit. The TOD Deed must be recorded during your lifetime to be effective.
  </div>

  <!-- Featured snippet callout -->
  <div class="bg-[#F0EDE8] border-l-4 border-[#B8860B] p-6 mb-4 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <p class="font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] leading-relaxed">
      A California Transfer on Death Deed (TOD Deed) is a revocable document that transfers real property to a named beneficiary automatically at the owner's death, without probate. It is established under California Civil Code §5600–5696 (operative January 1, 2022 under AB 1052) and can be revoked any time before death.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- E-E-A-T disclaimer -->
  <div class="bg-[#FAF8F5] border border-[#E5E0D8] rounded-lg p-4 mb-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto text-sm text-[#6B6B6B]">
    <strong class="text-[#3D3D3D]">Last updated July 2026.</strong> This information reflects California Civil Code §5600–5696 as operative under AB 1052 (January 1, 2022). Laws may change. Consult a licensed California attorney for advice specific to your situation.
  </div>

  <!-- H1 -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-8">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl text-[#1C1C1E] leading-tight">
      California Transfer on Death Deed: How to Set One Up, Record It, and Revoke It
    </h1>
  </div>

  <!-- Form app -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
    <AffidavitFormApp
      client:load
      documentType="tod-deed"
      state="California"
      waitingDays={0}
      statutoryReference="California Civil Code §5600–5696 (AB 1052, operative January 1, 2022)"
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Body content -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-4">What a California TOD Deed Is — and Why Searches for It Are Surging</h2>
    <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A Transfer on Death Deed lets a California homeowner name who will receive their property when they die — without a will, without a trust, and without probate. The property transfers automatically upon death, recorded at the county recorder's office.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Search interest in California TOD Deeds increased roughly 900% year-over-year after AB 1052 made California one of the first large states to formally authorize revocable TOD Deeds for real property in 2022. Before that legislation, California homeowners who wanted to avoid probate typically needed a living trust (usually $2,000–$5,000 to create) or joint tenancy (which carries its own complications).
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-4">How It Differs from a Will and a Trust</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5]">
            <th class="p-3 text-left">Feature</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-left">TOD Deed</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-left">Will</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-left">Revocable Living Trust</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Avoids probate</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#4A7C59] font-medium">✓ Yes</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#C0392B]">✗ No</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#4A7C59] font-medium">✓ Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8] bg-[#FAF8F5]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Attorney required</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">Not required</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">Recommended</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">Strongly recommended</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Cost to set up</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">~$15–$21 recording fee</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$1,000–$3,000+</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$2,000–$5,000+</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8] bg-[#FAF8F5]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Covers all assets</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">Real property only</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#4A7C59] font-medium">All assets</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#4A7C59] font-medium">All assets</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Takes effect</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">At death</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">After probate</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">At death</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-4">Key Features of the California TOD Deed</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
      <div class="border border-[#E5E0D8] rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#FAF8F5]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] mb-2">No asset threshold</h3>
        <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm">Can be used for any California real property, regardless of value. A $5 million home and a $200,000 rental both qualify.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-[#E5E0D8] rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#FAF8F5]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] mb-2">Revocable until death</h3>
        <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm">You retain complete ownership and control during your lifetime. Sell it, mortgage it, lease it — the beneficiary has no rights until you die.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-[#E5E0D8] rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#FAF8F5]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] mb-2">Multiple beneficiaries allowed</h3>
        <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm">Name more than one beneficiary (tenants in common, equal shares unless specified). Name alternates who take if a primary beneficiary predeceases you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-[#E5E0D8] rounded-xl p-4 bg-[#FAF8F5]">
        <h3 class="font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] mb-2">Must record within 60 days</h3>
        <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm">Recording must occur within 60 days of notarization (Civil Code §5628). An unrecorded TOD Deed is ineffective — the transfer will not occur at death.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-4">How to Complete a California TOD Deed</h2>

    <div class="space-y-4 mb-8">
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Gather what you need</strong>
          <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm mt-1">Your full legal name as it appears on the current deed. Full legal name of your beneficiary. The <strong>Assessor's Parcel Number (APN)</strong> for the property (from your tax bill or county assessor website). The legal description of the property (from the current deed). The county where the property is located.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Complete the form</strong>
          <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm mt-1">Fill in the grantor's full legal name, beneficiary name(s), property legal description, and APN. The deed must include statutory language under Civil Code §5642 stating the transfer is revocable and takes effect only at death.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Sign before a notary public</strong>
          <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm mt-1">The grantor (property owner) must sign in the presence of a notary public. The beneficiary does not need to sign and does not need to be notified during the grantor's lifetime.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">4</div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Record with the county recorder within 60 days</strong>
          <p class="text-[#3D3D3D] text-sm mt-1">File the notarized TOD Deed with the county recorder in the county where the property is located. Pay the per-page recording fee. <strong>Must be recorded within 60 days of notarization.</strong> An unrecorded TOD Deed is ineffective.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- County recorder fees -->
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-4">California County Recorder Fees (2026)</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5]">
            <th class="p-3 text-left">County</th>
            <th class="p-3 text-left">Fee Per Page</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Los Angeles County</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$21</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8] bg-[#FAF8F5]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">San Francisco County</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$15</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">San Diego County</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$21</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8] bg-[#FAF8F5]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Orange County</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$21</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#E5E0D8]">
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E]">Riverside County</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-[#3D3D3D]">$21</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-[#6B6B6B] mb-8">Most TOD Deeds are 2–3 pages. No documentary transfer tax is due on a TOD Deed during the grantor's lifetime.</p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-4">How to Revoke a California TOD Deed</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 mb-8">
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</div>
        <div><strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Complete a revocation deed</strong> — a document identifying the property and stating the TOD Deed is revoked</div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</div>
        <div><strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Sign before a notary public</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-lg border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#1C1C1E] text-[#FAF8F5] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center font-serif text-sm shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</div>
        <div><strong class="text-[#1C1C1E]">Record with the county recorder</strong> — revocation is only effective upon recording. Telling your beneficiary or crossing out the deed has no legal effect.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pb-16">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-[#1C1C1E] mb-8">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4">

      <details class="bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-xl border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          What if the beneficiary dies before I do?
          <span class="text-[#B8860B] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          If the named beneficiary predeceases you and you have not named an alternate beneficiary, the TOD Deed fails and the property passes according to your will (or, if no will, by intestate succession). Name an alternate beneficiary to avoid this.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-xl border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          Does a TOD Deed override my will?
          <span class="text-[#B8860B] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Generally, yes — for that specific property. The TOD Deed controls the disposition of the real property it covers regardless of what your will says about it. If you want a different distribution after you sign a TOD Deed, you must revoke it or execute a new one.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-xl border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          What if I have a mortgage?
          <span class="text-[#B8860B] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          The federal Garn-St. Germain Act protects against lender acceleration when property is transferred at death to an heir. Your lender generally cannot call the loan due because you died and the property transferred under the TOD Deed. However, the beneficiary should be prepared to either assume the mortgage (lender consent typically required) or refinance.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-xl border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          Can I have multiple beneficiaries?
          <span class="text-[#B8860B] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Yes. They take as tenants in common, each with an equal undivided share unless you specify percentages (e.g., 60% to one child, 40% to another). Co-owners must agree on management decisions after the grantor's death, which can create conflict.
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="bg-[#F0EDE8] rounded-xl border border-[#E5E0D8]">
        <summary class="p-6 font-medium text-[#1C1C1E] cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between">
          What happens after I die — does the beneficiary get the property immediately?
          <span class="text-[#B8860B] text-xl">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-[#3D3D3D] leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Not immediately. The beneficiary must wait 120 days (or until any will has been admitted to probate, whichever is later), then file an affidavit of survivorship with the county recorder along with a certified death certificate. The property is then recorded in the beneficiary's name.
        </div>
      </details>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Internal links -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pb-12 text-sm text-[#6B6B6B]">
    Related: <a href="/small-estate-affidavit/" class="text-[#B8860B] hover:underline">Small Estate Affidavit Hub</a> | <a href="/small-estate-affidavit/arizona/" class="text-[#B8860B] hover:underline">Arizona Small Estate Affidavit</a> | <a href="/" class="text-[#B8860B] hover:underline">Homepage</a>
  </div>

</StatePage>
